WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:14.230
DAVID J. HUNTER: So you discuss the difficulties

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:22.350
as a health care provider-- hot environment, protective clothing that

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:29.246
is heavy and leaves you dehydrated-- so you can only work one

00:00:29.246 --> 00:00:30.870
to one and one half hours at a stretch.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:38.820
How difficult does that make the interpersonal contact

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.880
with patients and just the technical ability

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:48.089
to do things like put in an intravenous syringe?

00:00:48.089 --> 00:00:50.380
RUPA KANAPATHIPILLAI: It's very challenging, obviously.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:59.100
The clinicians and I think most of the staff that worked in the ETUs

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:03.730
would do their best to try to engage with patients in a way that

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:10.270
is meaningful, so basic things like writing your name above your goggles

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:12.094
so patients could identify you.

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:16.480
This became a big part of it.

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:23.890
People were so afraid of being infected, being stuck in an ETU,

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:29.730
watching their loved ones die around them-- terrifying.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:34.810
So this became a big, very real practice for the clinicians.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:46.832
This was to try to provide some human familiarity, contact with the patients.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:56.380
In terms of doing procedures, it's incredibly challenging-- double gloves,

00:01:56.380 --> 00:02:04.320
you're dripping sweat from every pore, people are hypovolemic.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.050
This is incredibly challenging.

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:10.060
But you do your best.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:11.890
You try to find a big vein.

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:18.720
You don't attempt until you feel comfortable.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.719
It's incredibly challenging.

00:02:21.719 --> 00:02:23.760
DAVID J. HUNTER: So some have pointed to the fact

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.580
that the survival rates were so much higher

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:29.490
in people who had contracted Ebola or were

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:34.950
flown to Europe or the UK or the US.

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:38.350
The numbers are a couple of handfuls.

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:46.230
But it's been said that if those facilities were available,

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:50.430
the case fatality rate could have been so much lower.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:54.330
Is it really realistic to imagine that that

00:02:54.330 --> 00:03:03.160
could have been provided at short notice and the ability

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:07.350
to lower the case fatality rate could have

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:12.365
been put in place with more modern or more sophisticated

00:03:12.365 --> 00:03:16.780
ETUs supported from abroad?

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:20.580
RUPA KANAPATHIPILLAI: Again, there are several parts to this question.

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:24.900
I think what we know about-- what we've learned

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:28.680
from this outbreak is that factors that really contribute to mortality

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:34.050
include viral load at time of presentation and age.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:42.150
So that's one part of it, and then accounting

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:45.480
for other things like co-morbidities.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:50.700
So in the patients that were infected and then expatriated,

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:59.150
taken back to the US, to the UK, I think what

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:05.000
is important to remember is that they received a variety of interventions,

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:09.020
everything from mechanical ventilation to hemodialysis to a cocktail

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:13.360
of various experimental therapies.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:19.980
It is hard to know exactly which of those interventions, combined with age,

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:25.480
co-morbidities, viral load, impacted their outcomes.

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:28.680
The second part of it is in the midst of an outbreak,

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:41.390
to be able to implement very sophisticated medical devices is

00:04:41.390 --> 00:04:42.710
very, very challenging.

00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:45.620
At the peak of the outbreak, there was just

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:52.470
a need to do very basic things like provide enough ETU beds.

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:59.030
I think yes, optimistically, we would say forewarned is forearmed,

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:05.450
and I think having things in place like point-of-care tests, really simple,

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:10.850
basic tools that can be used in a more widespread way in ETUs

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:20.070
would perhaps help change outcomes in future outbreaks,

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:22.560
if there are future outbreaks.

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:29.140
Things like more invasive means to deliver

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:33.120
intravenous or intra-osseous resuscitation,

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:37.910
the use of inotropes, mechanical ventilation-- some of these things

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:41.120
I think perhaps can be implemented.

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:46.360
Some of these things are, I think, not real.

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:48.880
They stick in the peak of an outbreak.

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:59.050
Again, weighing up safety and feasibility in an ETU setting,

00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:02.230
I think, needs to be considered before we

00:06:02.230 --> 00:06:07.374
talk in more detail about more sophisticated mechanical tools.

00:06:07.374 --> 00:06:10.290
DAVID J. HUNTER: To say nothing about electricity and air conditioning

00:06:10.290 --> 00:06:13.960
and the other factors that you'd need.

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:21.250
In the ETU, you have patients-- you've now

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:24.800
got a four-hour turnaround on the test.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:29.530
How does one handle triage of who arrives,

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:34.330
who should be brought to the unit, who should be admitted,

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:38.620
and how do you deal with people with non-Ebola conditions,

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:43.360
but who are in urgent, acute need of medical care?

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:46.930
RUPA KANAPATHIPILLAI: So there is a fair system at emergency,

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:51.910
and the decision to admit someone to the suspect tent

00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:56.770
is really based on an assessment of risk, so fever plus,

00:06:56.770 --> 00:06:57.730
essentially, exposure.

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:05.920
So anyone that meets those triage admission criteria would get

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:12.220
admitted to the suspect tent or tents.

00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:15.520
They would then, if they had been symptomatic for greater than 72 hours,

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:20.950
have a PCR test with a four-hour turnaround time that would

00:07:20.950 --> 00:07:24.970
confirm yes or no, they were infected.

00:07:24.970 --> 00:07:29.590
If they were infected, they would go into the confirmed tent.

00:07:29.590 --> 00:07:33.430
Everyone that was admitted to the ETU would empirically

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:38.360
get broad-spectrum antibiotics and anti-malarials

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:46.376
to try to combat other common infectious diseases that may cause fever.

00:07:46.376 --> 00:07:50.770
So that was standard practice in our ETU.

00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:54.700
DAVID J. HUNTER: And people arriving with other treatable or social

00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:58.260
conditions just have to be turned away because your folks don't

00:07:58.260 --> 00:07:59.992
want a single disease.

00:07:59.992 --> 00:08:01.450
RUPA KANAPATHIPILLAI: That's right.

00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:04.750
So this is part of the complexity.

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:07.450
And one of the hardest things, in fact, is

00:08:07.450 --> 00:08:12.550
the people that have other clinical problems-- pregnant women-- this

00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:22.200
is one of the most tragic parts of at least my time in Liberia,

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:27.350
seeing pregnant women present who had been turned away

00:08:27.350 --> 00:08:32.090
from multiple other hospitals because they were febrile,

00:08:32.090 --> 00:08:36.590
they had exposure to Ebola.

00:08:36.590 --> 00:08:38.919
So the concern was of course that they were infected,

00:08:38.919 --> 00:08:48.130
and to have any sort of intervention with an associated enormous

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:55.700
transmission risk was not manageable in most

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:59.000
of the health care facilities in Liberia, at least at the time

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:01.290
that I was there.

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:03.750
DAVID J. HUNTER: And did that preexist the epidemic,

00:09:03.750 --> 00:09:10.560
or did those hospitals essentially close or fail

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:14.130
to function because of the fear of Ebola?

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:16.410
RUPA KANAPATHIPILLAI: So I think it's more the latter.

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:20.940
Once again, these are countries that don't have particularly strong health

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:25.150
systems to start with.

00:09:25.150 --> 00:09:30.190
You layer on top of that, really, the panic

00:09:30.190 --> 00:09:35.730
and the fear associated with acquiring Ebola or treating patients with Ebola,

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:38.970
fear of transmission to others.

00:09:38.970 --> 00:09:42.330
Health care in the country was essentially just shut down.

00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:46.180
There were a few functioning facilities.

00:09:46.180 --> 00:09:49.630
There was a referral hospital that we could refer patients to,

00:09:49.630 --> 00:09:55.780
but for many, many, many months, health care facilities that were not ETUs

00:09:55.780 --> 00:10:00.880
were completely shut down.

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:03.070
DAVID J. HUNTER: And this is a disease which

00:10:03.070 --> 00:10:07.810
may be one of the highest-risk groups for health care workers.

00:10:07.810 --> 00:10:14.480
I believe about 500 of the 11,000 people who died were health care workers.

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:17.950
So that impacts just the number of people available to help.

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:23.320
And what's the psychological impact for a health care worker

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:25.440
of knowing that and knowing that risk?

00:10:27.507 --> 00:10:29.590
RUPA KANAPATHIPILLAI: I can't speak for everybody.

00:10:29.590 --> 00:10:41.870
I think people's experiences have been quite varied,

00:10:41.870 --> 00:10:46.260
at least the people that I know and continue to be in contact with that

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:51.240
worked in an Ebola setting in this most recent outbreak,

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:58.240
and their responses have been really across the spectrum.

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:01.040
For some people, it's been incredibly challenging,

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:13.460
and for others, they have continued, and it's just been completely manageable.

00:11:13.460 --> 00:11:22.070
I think, of course, there is a concern of acquiring the disease, a fear,

00:11:22.070 --> 00:11:24.570
of course.

00:11:24.570 --> 00:11:29.270
In Medicin Sans Frontieres, it was very clear about rules and regulations

00:11:29.270 --> 00:11:35.490
in the field to try to minimize risk and try to mitigate that fear,

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:39.640
at least within our health care workers.

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:43.320
DAVID J. HUNTER: So you've been in other difficult places with MSF.

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:49.480
How is this different from working in other crises?

00:11:49.480 --> 00:11:54.107
RUPA KANAPATHIPILLAI: I have never quite experienced the level of fear.

00:11:57.240 --> 00:12:06.570
And again, I can't speak for everybody, but the level of panic

00:12:06.570 --> 00:12:11.280
I have never really experienced before in any setting.

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:18.275
I have never seen patients look so afraid or be so afraid.

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:33.290
I think that's probably the biggest thing, the fear of infection,

00:12:33.290 --> 00:12:40.620
of even the smallest contact with people, things that we take for granted

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:45.900
everyday like shaking hands-- just completely absent.

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:53.700
And I had worked in HIV for some years before.

00:12:56.420 --> 00:12:59.690
The only thing that I can possibly liken this to

00:12:59.690 --> 00:13:06.368
is perhaps the bad old days, the early days of HIV exactly before treatment.

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:13.980
The other thing that I think has been disturbing about this

00:13:13.980 --> 00:13:20.990
is the rapidity of deterioration of patients, hours to days as opposed

00:13:20.990 --> 00:13:23.890
to months.

